## 9. Storage

- **Where repositories are cloned**: `storage/repositories/<repositoryId>/`, with the base path read from an environment variable — `REPO_STORAGE_PATH` — never hardcoded. This one line of indirection is what makes moving to object storage in Phase 4 a config change instead of a code change in every module that touches file paths.
- **How storage is organized**: one folder per repository, named by its database ID, not by repo name (names collide, IDs don't). Add `storage/` to both `.gitignore` and `.dockerignore` immediately so a cloned repo never accidentally ends up in your own git history or Docker image.
- **What should be configurable via env vars**: the storage base path, clone timeout (seconds), max repo size, max concurrent clones (later, when workers exist). Anything that's a "limit" or a "location" belongs in config, not as a magic number buried in `GitService`.

---

## 10. AI Architecture (Design Only — Not Implemented Yet)

- **Where AI code lives**: entirely inside `ai/`. No other module imports an OpenAI, Gemini, or Anthropic SDK directly — ever. This is the single rule that keeps your AI provider replaceable.
- **How to keep providers replaceable**: define one interface — conceptually, "something that can turn text into a vector" (`embed`) and "something that can turn a conversation + context into an answer" (`chat`). Every concrete provider (`OpenAIProvider`, `GeminiProvider`, `ClaudeProvider`) implements that same shape. The rest of the app — `indexing/` and `chat/` — only ever calls the interface, never a specific provider. Switching providers becomes a one-line change in configuration, not a search-and-replace across the codebase.
- **How RAG fits in later**: `IndexingService` produces embeddings and stores them in pgvector. `ChatService`, when a user asks a question, embeds *the question* using the same `AIService.embed()`, searches pgvector for the closest chunks, and hands those chunks to `AIService.chat()` as context alongside the question. Retrieval and generation are two separate calls to the same abstraction — neither `ChatService` nor `IndexingService` needs to know which underlying provider is actually doing the work.

