## 12. Scaling — From Single Developer to Production

```
Single developer, local storage, synchronous clone   (Phase 1)
                        │
                        ▼
Async processing via queue + worker, real-time status  (Phase 2)
                        │
                        ▼
AI understands code via RAG, chat becomes real          (Phase 3)
                        │
                        ▼
Object storage, distributed autoscaled workers,
caching, multi-repo support, production scale           (Phase 4)
```

Why this order avoids rewrites:

- **Local storage → object storage** is safe to defer because every file path already goes through `utils/filesystem.ts` and `REPO_STORAGE_PATH`. Swapping the implementation behind that one utility, and pointing workers at S3/R2 instead of local disk, doesn't touch `RepositoryService`, `GitService`, or anything above them.
- **Synchronous clone → queued clone** is safe to defer because `GitService` was written as an async, HTTP-agnostic function from the start. Whether it's awaited directly or awaited inside a job handler is invisible to `GitService` itself.
- **Single worker → autoscaled worker pool** is safe to defer because a queue-consumer worker is inherently stateless per-job — scaling it from one process to many is an infrastructure decision (how many worker instances are running), not a code change.
- **No AI → RAG-based chat** is safe to defer because the `ai/` interface was defined as a contract before any provider existed, so `indexing/` and `chat/` can be built against that contract without caring which provider eventually implements it.

The throughline across all four phases: **every "later" problem was already given a seam to plug into, back when it was built.** That's what separates "we'll refactor when we get there" from "we designed for where we're going, without building it early." You've landed on the second one.

---

## Summary — The One Sentence Version

Each module does exactly one job, talks to its neighbors only through services (never through shared database access), and every place where "the simple version today" will later need to become "the scalable version tomorrow" already has a clean seam — a service boundary, an interface, or a config variable — ready for that change to slot into.